# Cursor Rules for Easy Homepage Project

- You are my favorite developer and you write very good and quality code.

## Tech Stack Requirements
- **Backend**: Node.js with Fastify framework
- **Frontend**: Tailwind CSS for all styling
- **Package Manager**: pnpm (preferred over npm/yarn)
- **Development Environment**: mise for tool version management

## Code Organization Principles
- Split code into logical, focused files to maintain readability
- Use clear separation of concerns
- Keep files small and focused on single responsibilities
- Organize code by feature/domain rather than by technical layer

## File Structure Guidelines

### Backend (Node.js + Fastify)
```
src/
├── routes/           # API route handlers
│   ├── auth.js
│   ├── users.js
│   └── sites.js
├── services/         # Business logic
│   ├── authService.js
│   ├── userService.js
│   └── siteService.js
├── models/           # Data models/schemas
│   ├── User.js
│   └── Site.js
├── middleware/       # Custom middleware
│   ├── auth.js
│   ├── validation.js
│   └── errorHandler.js
├── utils/            # Utility functions
│   ├── database.js
│   ├── validation.js
│   └── helpers.js
├── config/           # Configuration files
│   ├── database.js
│   └── app.js
└── app.js            # Main application entry point
```

### Frontend (HTML + Tailwind CSS)
```
public/
├── css/              # Custom CSS (minimal, prefer Tailwind)
│   └── custom.css
├── js/               # Client-side JavaScript
│   ├── components/
│   ├── utils/
│   └── main.js
└── views/            # HTML templates
    ├── layouts/
    ├── partials/
    └── pages/
```

## Coding Standards

### Node.js + Fastify
- Use ES6+ features (async/await, destructuring, arrow functions)
- Implement proper error handling with try-catch blocks
- Use Fastify's built-in validation and serialization
- Follow RESTful API conventions
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Implement proper logging with appropriate log levels

### Tailwind CSS
- Use Tailwind utility classes for all styling
- Avoid custom CSS unless absolutely necessary
- Use Tailwind's responsive prefixes (sm:, md:, lg:, xl:)
- Leverage Tailwind's color palette and spacing system
- Use Tailwind's component classes for complex UI patterns
- Prefer Tailwind's built-in animations and transitions
- **MANDATORY**: Implement dark mode toggle button in frontend
- Use Tailwind's dark mode classes (dark:) for theme switching
- Store theme preference in localStorage
- Provide smooth transitions between light and dark modes

### Code Splitting Rules
1. **Routes**: One file per resource/domain (auth, users, sites)
2. **Services**: One file per business domain with clear interfaces
3. **Models**: One file per data model with validation schemas
4. **Middleware**: One file per middleware concern
5. **Utils**: Group related utility functions in focused files
6. **Components**: Split UI components into separate files when they exceed 50 lines

## File Naming Conventions
- Use camelCase for JavaScript files
- Use kebab-case for HTML/CSS files
- Use descriptive names that indicate purpose
- Include file type in directory structure (services/, models/, etc.)

## Dependencies Management
- Use pnpm for package management
- Keep dependencies minimal and focused
- Prefer Fastify plugins over custom implementations
- Use Tailwind CSS via CDN or build process
- Document any non-standard dependencies

## Development Environment (mise)
- **MANDATORY**: Use mise for tool version management
- Create `.mise.toml` file to specify tool versions
- Use mise to manage Node.js, pnpm, and other development tools
- Ensure consistent development environment across team members
- Document required tool versions in mise configuration
- Use mise plugins for additional tools (Docker, databases, etc.)
- Run `mise install` to set up development environment
- Use `mise exec` for running commands with correct tool versions

## Error Handling
- Implement consistent error handling patterns
- Use Fastify's error handling mechanisms
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Log errors appropriately
- Handle both client and server-side errors gracefully

## Performance Guidelines
- Use Fastify's built-in performance optimizations
- Implement proper caching strategies
- Optimize Tailwind CSS builds
- Use appropriate HTTP status codes
- Implement proper request validation

## Security Considerations
- Use Fastify's security plugins
- Implement proper authentication/authorization
- Validate all inputs
- Use HTTPS in production
- Sanitize user inputs
- Implement rate limiting

## Testing Approach
- Write unit tests for services and utilities
- Test API endpoints with proper status codes
- Test error scenarios
- Use appropriate testing frameworks for Node.js
- Test Tailwind CSS responsive behavior

## Documentation Requirements
- **MANDATORY**: Create comprehensive README.md with clear examples
- Document what the application does with practical examples
- Include step-by-step local development setup instructions with mise
- Provide Docker setup and usage instructions with examples
- Document API endpoints with clear descriptions and examples
- Include examples for complex Tailwind CSS patterns
- Document any custom configurations
- Document environment variables and their purposes
- Include screenshots or GIFs showing dark/light mode functionality
- Provide troubleshooting section for common issues
- Document mise configuration and usage examples

## Code Review Checklist
- [ ] Code is split into logical, focused files
- [ ] Tailwind CSS is used for all styling
- [ ] Dark mode toggle button is implemented and functional
- [ ] Fastify best practices are followed
- [ ] Error handling is implemented
- [ ] Code is readable and well-organized
- [ ] Dependencies are minimal and necessary
- [ ] Security considerations are addressed
- [ ] Performance optimizations are in place
- [ ] README.md is comprehensive with examples
- [ ] Docker setup is documented and working
- [ ] mise configuration is properly set up
- [ ] Development environment is reproducible with mise
